  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n*** PATCHY FOG EARLY THURSDAY MORNING ***\n\nAt 245am Thursday morning, clouds are gradually pushing east of the \nI-65 corridor as drier air attempts to work its way into the region \nbehind a weak cold front. This has allowed patchy fog to develop in \nrural, low-lying areas where light winds have permitted temperatures \nto sink like a rock - into the mid 40s in spots. I lowered \ntemperatures and added some patchy fog to the forecast through 8am \nto account for this, but it should quickly dissipate thereafter as \nsurface heating eradicates PBL saturation. Also helping disperse any \nresidual morning fog will be increasing winds which mixing \ntechniques via Bufkit forecast soundings suggest may gust up to 20 \nmph late morning into the afternoon across northern Kentucky and \nespecially southern Indiana. Temperatures this afternoon will climb \ninto the low to mid 60s, which, though cooler than we've become used \nto recently, is near normal for early November in the Bluegrass \nstate.\n\n*** FROST AND FREEZE THIS WEEKEND ***\n\nCloud cover will increase again from the north tonight into tomorrow \nmorning as a cold core low dives southeast towards the area. \nConsequently, highs will run a few degrees cooler tomorrow, \nparticularly across areas near and north of the I-64 corridor where \nthe Rapid Refresh Ensemble Forecast System (REFS) mean brings 850mb \ntemperatures to < -2 degC. In addition, the RAP shows 0-1km RH > \n90%, light QPF, and a 0% probability of cloud ice tomorrow morning \ninto the early afternoon - implying we may see some patchy drizzle, \nif not true rain showers over the Bluegrass where weak diurnal \ninstability will develop by early afternoon. Highs in the upper 50s \nto low 60s will give way to Saturday morning lows in the 30s - \ncoolest over southern Indiana where clouds should depart by \nmidnight, allowing patchy frost to form.\n\nCool advection will continue through the day on Saturday, confining \nhighs to the 50s despite partly to mostly sunny skies. Then, with \nenough clearing, Saturday night lows will take a sharp dive, \nparticularly with high pressure settling over the area to promote \nlight-calm winds. The result will be area-wide frost and in some \nlocations, a freeze. NBM probabilities for sub 32 degF lows are \nhighest (60-90%) along two corridors: one over southern Indiana west \nof the Louisville Metro, and another across the Bluegrass. It is \nthis second area where a hard freeze may also be achieved, primarily \nin low-lying rural areas; NBM probabilities for such (< 28 degF) \ntemperatures are 30-50%. While the horticulture growing season is \nover, such freezing temperatures promote prussic acid formation in \ncertain plants - suggesting possible livestock impacts in the \nabsence of protective action. As a result, we will likely need \nfrost/freeze headlines to highlight this potential beginning \ntomorrow night and again on Saturday and/or Sunday nights.\n\nDry and mild weather is expected going into next work week, with \nafternoon highs in the 60s following crisp mornings in the 30s.",
